the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-288436/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-288436"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-287971"] :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-287970"] :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-288440/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e7b8ce83-4463-590d-b26c-44b7c9326b20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288440/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "04cd0694-9be7-5d1b-abc9-84c983507215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288440/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b5